r(--muted);font-size:.95rem;white-space:nowrap}main#main .tt-d{flex:0 0 auto;width:.7rem;height:.7rem;border-radius:50%}main#main .tt-d.sell{background:#3d6f93}main#main .tt-d.bal{background:#b59a77}main#main .tt-d.buy{background:#c4783a}@media (max-width:420px){main#main .tt{padding:.5rem .6rem .3rem}main#main .tt th,main#main .tt td{padding:.5rem .2rem;font-size:.95rem}main#main .tt td span{font-size:.88rem;gap:.25rem}main#main .tt tbody th{width:34%}}</style><div class="tt"><table><caption>Middle resale price and months of supply, by town</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">Town</th><th scope="col">Houses</th><th scope="col">Condos</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope="row"><a href="/submarkets/myrtle-beach/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">Myrtle Beach</a></th><td><b>$430,000</b><span><i class="tt-d bal" aria-hidden="true"></i>5 to 6 months</span></td><td><b>$172,000</b><span><i class="tt-d buy" aria-hidden="true"></i>about a year</span></td></tr><tr><th scope="row"><a href="/submarkets/north-myrtle-beach/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">North Myrtle Beach</a></th><td><b>$535,000</b><span><i class="tt-d bal" aria-hidden="true"></i>6.1 months</span></td><td><b>$300,000</b><span><i class="tt-d buy" aria-hidden="true"></i>7.5 months</span></td></tr><tr><th scope="row"><a href="/submarkets/surfside-beach/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">Surfside Beach</a></th><td><b>$620,000</b><span><i class="tt-d sell" aria-hidden="true"></i>3.6 months</span></td><td><b>$370,000</b><span>Too few sales</span></td></tr><tr><th scope="row"><a href="/submarkets/garden-city/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">Garden City</a></th><td><b>$385,000</b><span><i class="tt-d buy" aria-hidden="true"></i>9.4 months</span></td><td><b>$220,000</b><span><i class="tt-d bal" aria-hidden="true"></i>5.1 months</span></td></tr><tr><th scope="row"><a href="/submarkets/little-river/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">Little River</a></th><td><b>$350,000</b><span><i class="tt-d sell" aria-hidden="true"></i>3.7 months</span></td><td><b>$155,000</b><span><i class="tt-d buy" aria-hidden="true"></i>8.1 months</span></td></tr><tr><th scope="row"><a href="/submarkets/carolina-forest/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">Carolina Forest</a></th><td><b>$395,000</b><span><i class="tt-d sell" aria-hidden="true"></i>3.7 months</span></td><td><b>$172,000</b><span>Too few sales</span></td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Forestbrook</th><td><b>$331,000</b><span><i class="tt-d sell" aria-hidden="true"></i>3.1 months</span></td><td><span>Too few sales</span></td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Socastee</th><td><b>$292,000</b><span><i class="tt-d sell" aria-hidden="true"></i>4.0 months</span></td><td><b>$178,000</b><span>Too few sales</span></td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Red Hill</th><td><b>$310,000</b><span><i class="tt-d sell" aria-hidden="true"></i>3.7 months</span></td><td><b>$164,000</b><span>Too few sales</span></td></tr><tr><th scope="row"><a href="/submarkets/conway/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">Conway</a></th><td><b>$265,000</b><span><i class="tt-d sell" aria-hidden="true"></i>4.1 months</span></td><td><b>$164,000</b><span>Too few sales</span></td></tr></tbody></table></div><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Garden City has the most houses for sale against its sales, about 9 months, but more than half of them are manufactured homes. "Too few sales" marks a town with too few resales of that kind to count.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Each town's guide has its own streets, prices and rules. Our <a href="/market-reports/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">market reports</a> follow prices month by month.</p><div class="bx"><p class="bx-t">How much room is there on the home you like?</p><p>Send us the address. An agent pulls the nearby sales and how long homes like it have sat on the market, so you know where to start your offer.</p><p class="bx-b"><a class="btn btn-brass" href="/contact/" data-topic="buying-in-myrtle-beach-offer">Have us price one home</a></p></div></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory-2)" id="prices"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3" data-toc="What homes cost">How much does a house or condo cost in Myrtle Beach?</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">The middle resale house or other home in Horry County sold for about $350,000 in the past year, and the middle resale condo for about $215,000. Half sold for more, half for less.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">New homes are a big part of the market. Builders sold more than 1 in 4 of the homes recorded in the county. The middle home a builder sold, condos aside, went for about $330,000. New houses have the fewest months of supply of any kind of home here, about 3. Our <a href="/buyers/new-construction/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">new construction guide</a> covers buying from a builder.</p><div class="c3-aerial"><figure class="c3ph-hero" style=""><div class="c3ph-f"><img src="/images/2026-11-a/buyers-cost-to-own/carolina-forest-house-streets-from-above-1200w.webp" srcset="/images/2026-11-a/buyers-cost-to-own/carolina-forest-house-streets-from-above-600w.webp 600w, /images/2026-11-a/buyers-cost-to-own/carolina-forest-house-streets-from-above-1200w.webp 1200w" sizes="(max-width: 1200px) 100vw, 1136px" width="1200" height="900" alt="Rows of two-story houses with gray roofs and short driveways along curving streets and a cul-de-sac, beside ponds and pine woods, seen from above" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><span class="c3ph-cr"><a href="https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/USCODE-2023-title17/html/USCODE-2023-title17-chap1-sec105.htm" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" aria-label="U.S. government aerial photo. License: Public domain" title="License: Public domain">U.S. government aerial photo</a></span></div><figcaption>Houses on streets in Carolina Forest, outside city limits, seen from above. The middle resale house there sold for about $395,000.</figcaption></figure></div><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">What does a home cost each year besides the mortgage?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">The middle house, about $350,000, costs about $7,850 a year besides the mortgage if you live in it and it is outside city limits. That is about $650 a month: tax about $1,290, the county stormwater fee $89, insurance about $2,000, HOA about $1,200 and upkeep about $3,270.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">The middle resale condo, about $215,000, costs about $10,170 a year, and three quarters of that is the condo fee. In Myrtle Beach and North Myrtle Beach the middle condo costs more to own each year than the middle house.</p><style>main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact{padding-top:1rem;padding-bottom:.85rem}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact .cap{margin-bottom:.7rem}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact [role=rowgroup]{gap:.5rem}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact [role=row]{gap:.1rem .9rem}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact [role=rowheader]{line-height:1.3}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact .b{height:.5rem;min-width:.5rem}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact .v{line-height:1.25}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact figcaption{margin-top:.8rem}@media (max-width:699px){main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact [role=rowgroup]{gap:.45rem}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact [role=row]{gap:.05rem}}</style><figure class="c3-gchart c3-gchart-compact"><div role="table" aria-label="What the middle condo and house cost to own a year, besides the mortgage"><span class="cap" aria-hidden="true">What the middle condo and house cost to own a year, besides the mortgage</span><div role="rowgroup" class="sr"><div role="row"><span role="columnheader">Home, middle resale price</span><span role="columnheader">yearly cost</span></div></div><div role="rowgroup"><div role="row"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">Condo in Myrtle Beach</span><span class="dot" aria-hidden="true">&middot;</span><span class="f">middle price $172,000</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.87" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">$11,490</span></span></div><div role="row" class="ref"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">House in Myrtle Beach</span><span class="dot" aria-hidden="true">&middot;</span><span class="f">middle price $430,000</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.621" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">$8,210</span></span></div><div role="row"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">Condo in North Myrtle Beach</span><span class="dot" aria-hidden="true">&middot;</span><span class="f">middle price $300,000</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.851" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">$11,240</span></span></div><div role="row" class="ref"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">House in North Myrtle Beach</span><span class="dot" aria-hidden="true">&middot;</span><span class="f">middle price $535,000</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.581" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">$7,670</span></span></div></div></div><figcaption>Dollars a year, as your home: property tax, the county fee where it applies, insurance, the HOA or condo fee and upkeep. The condo fee is most of a condo's cost.</figcaption></figure><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">A low price does not mean a low yearly cost: condo owners pay the fee for the building they share. Our <a href="/buyers/cost-to-own/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">cost to own guide</a> has every part, town by town, and our <a href="/invest/condos/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">condo investing page</a> covers renting one out.</p></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory)" id="steps"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3" data-toc="The steps">What are the steps to buy a house in South Carolina?</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Eight steps, from choosing an agent to the deed recorded in Conway. The biggest difference from many states: every South Carolina home closing must be supervised by a South Carolina attorney.</p><style>main#main .bs{list-style:none;margin:1.2rem 0 1.4rem;padding:0;max-width:760px;position:relative}main#main .bs::before{content:"";position:absolute;left:1.05rem;top:.6rem;bottom:.6rem;width:2px;background:rgba(28,32,40,.16)}main#main .bs>li{position:relative;margin:0 0 .7rem;padding:0 0 0 3.1rem}main#main .bs .n{position:absolute;left:0;top:.75rem;display:inline-flex;align-items:center;justify-content:center;width:2.15rem;height:2.15rem;border-radius:50%;background:var(--navy);color:#fff;font-family:var(--sans);font-weight:700;font-size:.95rem}main#main .bs li.hot .n{background:#c4783a}main#main .bs .c{display:block;padding:.85rem 1rem .9rem;border-radius:16px;background:rgba(255,255,255,.62);border:1px solid rgba(255,255,255,.8);box-shadow:0 10px 26px -20px rgba(28,32,40,.35);color:var(--muted);line-height:1.6}main#main .bs li.hot .c{border:2px solid #c4783a;background:#fff}main#main .bs h3{display:block;color:var(--navy)!important;font-family:var(--sans)!important;font-size:1.1rem!important;line-height:1.35;margin:0 0 .3rem!important;font-weight:700;text-wrap:balance}main#main .bs p{margin:0 0 .45rem;max-width:none}main#main .bs p:last-child{margin-bottom:0}main#main .bs .sr{position:absolute;width:1px;height:1px;overflow:hidden;clip:rect(0 0 0 0);white-space:nowrap}@media (max-width:599px){main#main .bs::before{display:none}main#main .bs>li{padding:0}main#main .bs .n{left:.9rem;top:.8rem;width:1.9rem;height:1.9rem;font-size:.9rem}main#main .bs h3{display:flex!important;align-items:center;min-height:1.9rem;padding-left:2.5rem}main#main .bs .c{padding:.8rem .95rem .85rem}}</style><ol class="bs"><li><div class="c"><h3><span class="n">1</span><span class="sr"> Step 1: </span>Sign a buyer agreement with your agent</h3><p>In South Carolina, no firm represents you as your agent until you both sign a written agreement that says so. Agents who use the MLS must sign one with you before a tour. It states the agent's pay, and that pay is negotiable, not fixed by law.</p></div></li><li><div class="c"><h3><span class="n">2</span><span class="sr"> Step 2: </span>Choose a home and make an offer</h3><p>Before you offer, we find the home's flood zone and get an insurance quote for the address, if you buy with Chapter3. Under South Carolina law an electronic signature counts the same as ink, so you can sign the offer from anywhere.</p><p>When the brokerage holds your earnest money check, it goes into the firm's trust account within two business days of the accepted offer. It stays there until the sale closes or ends.</p></div></li><li><div class="c"><h3><span class="n">3</span><span class="sr"> Step 3: </span>Inspect the home and read the papers</h3><p>A home inspection is the buyer's job in South Carolina. Under the seller's disclosure law the buyer still has the duty to inspect, and agents have no duty to inspect. We line up the inspection.</p><p>On most resales of a house with one to four units, the seller must give the buyer the state's written disclosure form before the buyer signs. The contract can say otherwise. The termite letter, called the CL-100, is a state form only a licensed pest control applicator may issue. For a condo, we get the HOA papers and the rental rules.</p></div></li><li><div class="c"><h3><span class="n">4</span><span class="sr"> Step 4: </span>The loan and the appraisal</h3><p>Your lender must give you a Loan Estimate within three business days of your application. In our experience, low appraisals are rare here. When one comes in low, we send comparable sales with a request for a reconsideration of value.</p><p>You get a free copy of the appraisal as soon as it is done, and no later than three business days before closing. You can agree in advance to get it at closing instead.</p></div></li><li class="hot"><div class="c"><h3><span class="n">5</span><span class="sr"> Step 5: </span>The closing attorney</h3><p>A South Carolina attorney supervises the title search, the loan papers, the closing and the recording of the deed and mortgage. With a home loan for personal or family use, the lender must ask which attorney you want and follow your choice.</p><p>We work with several closing attorneys and can recommend them once we are working with you. Your lender's Closing Disclosure arrives at least three business days before you close.</p></div></li><li><div class="c"><h3><span class="n">6</span><span class="sr"> Step 6: </span>Closing, here or from home</h3><p>You can sign at the attorney's office. From another state, your mortgage and other papers can be signed before a notary where you live, with two witnesses.</p><p>South Carolina law has no remote, or video, notarization: a South Carolina notary and the signer must be in each other's physical presence.</p></div></li><li><div class="c"><h3><span class="n">7</span><span class="sr"> Step 7: </span>The deed is recorded in Conway</h3><p>The deed and mortgage are recorded at the Horry County Register of Deeds in Conway. Recording costs $15 for a deed and $25 for a mortgage; both fees are in state law.</p><p>The seller pays the state deed fee of $3.70 for every $1,000 of the price. Our <a href="/buyers/closing-costs/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">closing costs page</a> lists the buyer's side.</p></div></li><li class="hot"><div class="c"><h3><span class="n">8</span><span class="sr"> Step 8: </span>Apply for the 4 percent tax rate</h3><p>A home you live in and apply for is taxed on 4 percent of its value; a second home or a rental on 6 percent. The 4 percent rate is not automatic: you apply after you buy.</p><p>On a house the county values at $350,000, the 2026 tax is about $1,290 a year at 4 percent and about $4,220 at 6 percent outside the cities. Inside the City of Myrtle Beach it is about $1,410 and about $5,350. In 2025 the county asked new owners to apply by May 31. A sale resets the county's value, so the seller's tax bill is not yours. Our <a href="/buyers/property-taxes/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">property tax guide</a> has the form.</p></div></li></ol><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">How long does it take to close? As long as the closing date you and the seller write into the contract. With a loan, two waits come from federal rules: the Loan Estimate and the Closing Disclosure, three business days each.</p><div class="bx"><p class="bx-t">Buying from another state?</p><p>Tell us where you live and what you want. We line up the inspection, the insurance quote and a closing attorney, and the papers can be signed where you live.</p><p class="bx-b"><a class="btn btn-brass" href="/contact/" data-topic="buying-in-myrtle-beach-steps">Ask us to plan your purchase</a></p></div></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory-2)" id="out-of-state"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3" data-toc="Buying from out of state">Can I buy a home in Myrtle Beach from out of state?</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Yes, and many buyers do. About 3 in 10 homes sold in the past year went to a buyer whose tax bill goes to another state or country; for condos, more than half.</p><style>main#main .bo{list-style:none;margin:1.1rem 0 1.3rem;padding:0;display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(2,minmax(0,1fr));gap:.65rem;max-width:620px}main#main .bo li{margin:0;padding:1rem 1rem .95rem;border-radius:18px;background:linear-gradient(160deg,rgba(42,48,64,.96),rgba(28,32,40,.98));color:rgba(244,239,232,.92);line-height:1.45;font-family:var(--sans);font-size:1rem}main#main .bo strong{display:block;color:#d4894a;font-family:var(--serif);font-size:clamp(1.7rem,6.5vw,2.2rem);line-height:1.05;margin-bottom:.35rem;font-weight:600}</style><ul class="bo" role="list"><li><strong>3 in 10</strong>homes sold went to a buyer with a tax bill in another state or country</li><li><strong>Over half</strong>of condos sold went to such a buyer</li></ul><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">North Carolina sends the most out-of-state buyers by far, then New York, Pennsylvania, New Jersey and Virginia. In the same building or subdivision, buyers with an out-of-state tax bill paid the same middle price as South Carolina buyers.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">More people keep coming. The Myrtle Beach metro grew faster than every U.S. metro but one in the year to July 2025. About 39 more people a day moved into Horry County from the rest of the country than moved out.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">From another state, you sign the offer electronically (step 2) and the closing papers before a notary where you live (step 6). Our <a href="/buyers/relocating/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">relocation guide</a> covers the move itself.</p></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory)" id="what-can-go-wrong"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3" data-toc="What can go wrong">What can go wrong when buying in Myrtle Beach?</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Five things here surprise buyers from other states. Each has a fix, and we handle most of them before you offer.</p><style>main#main .br{list-style:none;margin:1.1rem 0 1.4rem;padding:0;display:grid;gap:.65rem;max-width:860px}@media (min-width:760px){main#main .br{grid-template-columns:repeat(2,minmax(0,1fr))}main#main .br li:last-child:nth-child(odd){grid-column:1/-1}}main#main .br li{margin:0;padding:1rem 1.1rem;border-radius:18px;background:rgba(255,255,255,.62);border:1px solid rgba(255,255,255,.8);box-shadow:0 10px 26px -20px rgba(28,32,40,.35);color:var(--muted);line-height:1.6}main#main .br h3{display:block;color:var(--navy)!important;font-family:var(--sans)!important;font-size:1.1rem!important;line-height:1.35;margin:0 0 .3rem!important;font-weight:700}main#main .br p{margin:0;max-width:none}main#main .br p.w{margin-top:.55rem;padding-top:.55rem;border-top:1px solid rgba(28,32,40,.1);color:var(--navy);font-weight:600}main#main .br p.w a{font-weight:600}</style><ul class="br" role="list"><li><h3>Flood zone</h3><p>About 1 in 4 condos and 1 in 15 houses resold in Horry County in the past year sat in FEMA's high-risk flood zone. There, a loan from a federally regulated lender needs flood insurance.</p><p class="w">We find the zone before you offer. Our <a href="/buyers/undisclosed-flooding/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">flood history guide</a> shows what else we look for.</p></li><li><h3>Insurance</h3><p>A one-family house owner here pays a middle of about $2,000 a year for home insurance, about 40 percent more than in the rest of South Carolina. A condo owner pays about $800 for their own policy.</p><p class="w">We get a quote for the address before you offer, if you buy with us. Our <a href="/buyers/coastal-insurance/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">coastal insurance guide</a> explains wind and flood cover.</p></li><li><h3>The second-home tax rate</h3><p>If you never apply for the 4 percent rate, your home is taxed at 6 percent, as a second home is: about three times the tax (step 8).</p><p class="w">We work out the tax at both rates on any address you send.</p></li><li><h3>Nightly rentals</h3><p>Inside the City of Myrtle Beach, a rental under 90 days is legal only where the zoning allows it. About 3 in 4 condos are in such zones; about 96 in 100 houses are not. In North Myrtle Beach a short-term rental needs a business license, and for now there is no special zoning or permit process beyond it.</p><p class="w">We read the zoning and the HOA papers before you offer. Our <a href="/invest/str-rules/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">short-term rental rules page</a> covers each town.</p></li><li><h3>Condos run like hotels</h3><p>Fannie Mae will not back a loan in a building run like a hotel. Examples: a front desk, daily cleaning, or a rule that owners join the rental program.</p><p class="w">Our <a href="/invest/condotel-financing/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">condotel loan guide</a> explains the other loans, and our <a href="/buyers/condo-in-litigation/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">condo litigation page</a> covers buildings in a lawsuit.</p></li></ul><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Our <a href="/buyers/common-mistakes/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">buyer mistakes page</a> ranks the ten that cost the most, with what each one costs on a typical house.</p></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory-2)" id="buying-in-myrtle-beach-faq"><div class="wrap"><p style="font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.7rem;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--brass);margin-bottom:.5rem;font-weight:600">Common questions</p><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3">Buying in Myrtle Beach FAQ</h2><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">Is Myrtle Beach a buyer's market?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">For condos, yes: about 7.5 months of supply, past the 5 to 7 months of a balanced local market. For houses, not yet: about 4 months. Houses at $700,000 and up are near the point where buyers gain the edge.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">Will home prices in Myrtle Beach go down?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">We do not forecast prices. The federal house price index for Horry County was about flat in the year to mid-2026, after rising about 51 percent from mid-2021 to mid-2025. More than half of the homes for sale have cut their asking price.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">Do you need a lawyer to buy a house in South Carolina?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Yes. Every South Carolina home closing must be supervised by a South Carolina attorney. With a home loan for personal or family use, the lender must ask which attorney you want and follow your choice.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">How long does it take to close on a house in South Carolina?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">As long as the closing date you and the seller write into the contract. With a loan, federal rules add two waits: the Loan Estimate within three business days of applying, and the Closing Disclosure at least three business days before closing.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">What is a CL-100?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">The termite letter, the Official South Carolina Wood Infestation Report. Only a licensed pest control applicator may issue it for a sale or a loan. It reports visible insect damage, active infestations, and moisture and decay under the house.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">What time of year do the fewest homes sell in Myrtle Beach?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">January and February. Recorded resales were fewest then, about 430 houses a month, and were about 620 to 670 a month from May to October.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">Can I rent my Myrtle Beach condo by the night?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Inside the City of Myrtle Beach, about 3 in 4 condos are in zones that allow rentals under 90 days. In North Myrtle Beach a short-term rental needs a business license. A condo's own rules can still ban them.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">What is the difference between a condo and a condotel?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">A condotel is a condo in a building run like a hotel: a front desk, daily cleaning or a rule that owners join the rental program, for example. Fannie Mae will not back a loan there.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">How much are property taxes in Myrtle Beach?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">On a house the county values at $350,000, the 2026 tax inside the City of Myrtle Beach is about $1,410 a year if you live in it. As a second home it is about $5,350. Outside the cities it is about $1,290 and $4,220.</p><p style="font-size:.78rem;color:var(--muted);line-height:1.7;max-width:760px;margin-top:1.6rem"><strong style="color:var(--navy)">Sources:</strong> <a href="https://acclaimweb.horrycounty.org/AcclaimWeb/search/SearchTypeDocType" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Horry County deed index</a>, <a href="https://trerc.tamu.edu/wp-content/uploads/files/PDFs/Articles/1897.pdf" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Texas A&M Real Estate Research Center</a>, <a href="https://www.fhfa.gov/data/hpi" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">FHFA house price index</a>, <a href="https://www.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest.html" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Census population estimates</a>, <a href="https://www.scstatehouse.gov/code/t40c057.php" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">SC Code 40-57</a>, <a href="https://www.scstatehouse.gov/code/t27c050.php" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">SC Code 27-50</a>, <a href="https://www.sccourts.org/media/opinions/HTMLFiles/SC/25508.htm" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Doe v. McMaster</a>, <a href="https://www.consumerfinance.gov/ask-cfpb/what-is-a-loan-estimate-en-1995/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">CFPB</a>, <a href="https://www.scstatehouse.gov/code/t08c021.php" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">SC Code 8-21</a>, <a href="https://www.scstatehouse.gov/coderegs/Chapter%2027.pdf" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">SC Regulation 27-1085</a>, <a href="https://www.scstatehouse.gov/code/t12c043.php" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">SC Code 12-43</a>, <a href="https://selling-guide.fanniemae.com/sel/b4-2.1-03/ineligible-projects" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Fannie Mae B4-2.1-03</a>. Months of supply: homes for sale in the Chapter3 listing feed, counted in total, against deeds recorded October 2025 to September 2026, builder sales included. Not legal or tax advice.</p><div class="c3ph-credits"><div class="c3ph-h" id="photo-credits">Photo credits</div><div role="list" aria-labelledby="photo-credits"><div role="listitem">The beach beside the SkyWheel in downtown Myrtle Beach. Photo: Tom Long, <a href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/2.0/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">CC BY-SA 2.0</a>, via <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Myrtle_Beach_(24050832475).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Wikimedia Commons</a>. Chapter3 Realty resized the photo and cut 1x1, 4x3 and 16x9 crops from it.</div><div role="listitem">Houses on streets in Carolina Forest, outside city limits, seen from above. Photo: NOAA National Geodetic Survey, <a href="https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/USCODE-2023-title17/html/USCODE-2023-title17-chap1-sec105.htm" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Public domain</a>, via <a href="https://noaa-eri-pds.s3.amazonaws.com/index.html#2026_Pre_Event/EC2601a_OB_N_RGB/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">NOAA Emergency Response Imagery</a>. Chapter3 Realty cut a 1080 x 810 px view from one NOAA map tile, raised the contrast and color slightly, and resized it to 1200 x 900.</div><div role="listitem">Chapter3 Realty resized these photos and cropped some of them. Each photo keeps the license listed with it.</div></div></div><script type="application/